worked_examples.solution:
(a) Test 1 shows the solid is a carbonate (effervescence; gas turns limewater milky). (b) Test 2 shows no halide (chloride/bromide/iodide) is present;

acidification was essential because the solid contains carbonate (Test 1), which would otherwise form a precipitate with silver nitrate and could be mistaken for a halide.

(c) The solid is most likely a carbonate salt of a metal that does not also contain a halide ion.

theory_blocks.body:
Two further details matter in practice. First, iron(II)'s green precipitate can slowly develop a brown tinge at its surface on standing in air, which should not be mistaken for a fresh iron(III) result — the test is read from the precipitate's colour as it first forms, not after it has been left standing.

Second, [[blue:'insoluble in excess' is itself a genuine, positive observation]] (for calcium, iron(II) and iron(III)), not a 'nothing happened' result to be skipped over;

the absence of a change in excess is exactly as diagnostic as a change would be for zinc or copper(II).

